ing about and the the  competencies that scribes are supposed  to develop for these modules yes these  and this is looking at the immediate  future because we know that the EHR is  not really going to get to a point where  it can just be the provider for awhile I  mean I'm not sure about the exact  forecast for that but I know it's out  there and I know that people are also  looking out for that to happen so in  that it's looking at once that once the  EHR is at that level where providers  don't necessarily need an assistant to  input that what then does the role of  the scribe become are they obsolete or  is there still room for them to assist  with documentation or  the betterment of the healthcare process  in some regard and if I if I can put  this in a little more context I was not  the only one selecting the expert panel  everyone on our research team helped and  in particular Jeff Gould and Vishnu but  also Dean city for example I asked some  people who know a lot of people we  started with a list we wanted some  professional associations represented so  we asked someone from the AMA and we got  a vice president to come who christine  sinski who has written quite a bit about  scribing we wanted someone from the  double-a MC etc etc then we wanted  providers we wanted representatives of  scribes we wanted industry and that was  so interesting because we did get a  representative from scribe America and a  representative from scribe X we so we  did invite about 30 people from all  different roles to be part of the expert  panel and to go back even further the  original list of competencies was based  on the field work we had done at five  sites around the country where we were  gathering best practices and we did our  analysis of all of the interviews and  field notes looking for competencies  there we did a literature search about  competencies and then we had a  conference and at the conference and it  was the same expert so they they know  who they are but they don't know what  input they're giving during these  distance survey pieces so at the  conference and he knows a lot about this  because he got to analyze the conference  proceedings which are crazy when you've  got 30 experts talking and for each of  the competences when he put it into the  survey  he read the transcripts to see what they  said so that the wording would be as  they wanted it and not as I wanted it or  as Keaton wanted it so I hope that helps  yeah thanks for clarifying  so I mean this entire thing could be  going way over my head so just stop me  if I'm asking a terrible question but  I'm curious how does like the time scale  of your study affect how likely like  your experts are to conform like well I  know it's like anonymous but like how  like how many rounds did you have to go  through like does that make sense like  yeah no that's that's a really good  question and an attrition of experts is  a really big deal in any study but with  the Delphi study you're really lucky to  have these experts that have that are  given their time to the study so you do  want to treat them actually really well  when you're sending out these surveys so  the first thing is that the surveys are  meticulously designed so that it's the  least amount of effort on the people  that are filling them out so that means  that that you really have to be  streamlined with it so it doesn't take  too much of their time because otherwise  they're less likely to finish it and  you're less likely to get the data but  after that so it's the first round and  the second round of the mandatory ones  and you do ask them for their reasoning  behind their choices so that you can  collect some of that data too but you're  absolutely right you don't want to  overburden your experts here with  unnecessary steps and you won't keep  repeating this round after round after  round after a certain point you've  collected the data you can and people  are set in their ways and you're not  just aiming for concordance the whole  time that's that's the happy result that  happens if you don't establish  concordance then you still accumulated  hopefully a lot of qualitative data to  look through anyway but you shouldn't be  exhausting your experts to the point  where they're out  all right well thank you so much I sold  [Applause]
 

